6. Apple port additions

Files added for the Apple (iPhone, iPad, Vision Pro, Apple TV and Mac) port by
its contributors are licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0
(`LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt`). This covers `tools/**`, `native/ios/**`,
`native/tvos/WA2LocalStorage.mm`, `tests/**`, `assets/app_icon/**` (original
artwork containing no game material) and the port documentation
(`README.md`, `README_EN.md`, `APPLE_PORT.md`, `APPLE_PORT_HISTORY.md`,
`APPLE_TV.md`).

Two files are derived from MIT-licensed upstream code and are provided under
the MIT License of their upstream projects instead:
`native/tvos/godot-tvos-17-remote.patch` (Godot Engine, `LICENSES/Godot-MIT.txt`)
and `native/tvos/godot_cpp_tools/tvos.py` (godot-cpp,
`addons/wmv_video/licenses/godot-cpp-MIT.txt`).

`assets/legal/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt` is generated by `tools/make_notices.py`
from the license texts it reproduces; each part keeps its own license.

7. FFmpeg and the GNU LGPL

The applications built from this repository use FFmpeg 7.1.5, licensed under
the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or later
(`LICENSES/LGPL-2.1-or-later.txt`). FFmpeg is configured with LGPL components
only (no `--enable-gpl`, `--enable-nonfree` or `--enable-version3`). The Mac
application ships FFmpeg as replaceable dynamic libraries. The iPhone/iPad/
Vision Pro and Apple TV applications link FFmpeg statically.

To meet the LGPL's terms for that static linking, the maintainers of this
repository:

- publish the complete source code of the application, its native plugin and
  every build script here, so a recipient can rebuild and relink it with a
  modified FFmpeg (see "FFmpeg and the LGPL" in `README.md`);
- attach the corresponding unmodified FFmpeg source archive to every published
  release, alongside the exact configure options in `tools/build_ios_ffmpeg.py`
  and `addons/wmv_video/BUILDING.md`;
- show the FFmpeg notice and the full LGPL text in each application
  ("Licenses" on the launcher) and in every release package; and
- permit recipients to modify the application for their own use and to
  reverse engineer it for debugging such modifications. Neither the
  Apache-2.0 nor the MIT terms used in this repository restrict this.

